description.narrative:
The mangrove ecosystem in Ecuador fulfills strategic functions for economic development and ecosystem services, as well as for the edification of local cultural expressions and the social fabric. Their strategic location between the sea and the land favors their high productivity, they fulfill multiple functions, they are indispensable for the development of aquatic species of nutritional and commercial value, they sustain the economy of coastal communities through fishing, wood and charcoal production. In addition to the environmental services, they provide carbon sequestration, buffering storms, control of coastal erosion and saline intrusion, they function as biological filters ensuring the continuity of life processes in the sea.
The mangrove territory is one of the most affected by climate change in Ecuador, with long-term effects on food security, economic activity of the communities, biological diversity, and ecosystem services. These effects of climate change, such as rising sea levels, colder and rainier winters and hotter and drier summers, are compounded by urban development, overexploitation of natural resources, dredging, landfill, water pollution from herbicides, clear-cutting, aquaculture and overfishing.
Climate change affects women disproportionately due to their dependence on natural resources, deepening gender inequality. This is the case of shell fisherwomen that are head of household and have establish their economy by fishing and marketing their fishing products. There are also the differentiated impacts of violence on women that increase their vulnerability during fishing activities, including the increasing violence resulting from drug trafficking. This activity requires immersion in territory without protection or redress measures, with the consequences of racial and spatial segregation, lower price for their work, difficulties for safe transportation, clandestinity of the activity, exposure of their lives and reputation.
During the year 2024, UNESCO and its partners implemented the School of Women Fisherwomen and Fisherwomen Rhizomes of Life through an educational platform that benefited 22 women from the communities of the mangrove territory, with the objective of strengthening local capacities to address the effects of climate change and violence in the territory, incorporating a strategic approach in the creation of safe spaces. This first phase of the project benefited 100 families in the mangrove estuaries of the Chone river, the Guayas delta and the Muisne Cojimíes estuary and the coastal marine area of the Macizo del Cajas Biosphere Reserve.
This intervention proposes a second phase of the School of Women Collectors and Fisherwomen Rhizomes of Life, with the objective of strengthening management and practical skills for the restoration of the mangrove ecosystem, as well as strengthening leadership skills for safe community spaces.
As a GEM 3 project, the results will address the structural causes of gender inequality in the mangrove ecosystem located on the coast of Ecuador, through local solutions fostered by women as key agents in processes of building knowledge and practices relevant to the sustainable development of the community. Women's participation enriches the approach to the effects of climate change and violence. It also promotes positive processes for the safety and care of people that affect the conditions of the environment to sustain quality life projects. This project not only aims to place women at the center of social change, but also to work on the structural causes that foster the disproportionate impact of climate change and violence on women. The intervention integrates several elements to advance gender equality at multiple levels:

1)  Women as key agents for social change: relevant knowledge and practices of women for local sustainable development; 

2) Co-creation of local solutions for social innovation with a gender approach: co-creation of a methodology for community safe spaces; 

3) Women's leadership and empowerment: exploring and proposing contextualized solutions that enable the reduction of gender inequalities while sustaining community resources to address climate change and violence; 

4) Knowledge for meaningful interventions: symbiosis between women's scientific and ancestral knowledge in favor of environmental action, through the provision of tools, methodologies and scientific knowledge to appropriate their local context.
